# Expectation record for 13_floating_point/004_long_double_target_restricted.c
#
# Reproduce one cell by hand with no harness: render the three command templates
# below, substituting the target triple, the optimization level, the source path,
# the output path, and the target's runner (empty on the natively executing target).

program            = 004_long_double_target_restricted
area               = 13_floating_point
description        = long double arithmetic, conversions and comparisons in a folded and a volatile-runtime variant, written and run on all four targets at all three optimization levels, with cross-backend value equality alone excluded because the type's representation was measured to differ across the four backends
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = disabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
This program contains no undefined and no unspecified behaviour.

EVERY VALUE IS EXACTLY REPRESENTABLE IN double, AND THAT IS THE PRECONDITION THE WHOLE PROGRAM RESTS
ON. Every operand and every result is a dyadic rational whose significand fits in 53 bits, so it is
exact in binary64 and therefore exact in the 64-bit x87 significand and the 113-bit binary128
significand alike. That is what allows one golden record to hold for four backends whose long double
formats differ, and it is asserted on the target rather than assumed: the exact_in_double line prints,
per value, whether the result survives a round trip through double unchanged, and every field is 1. A
carelessly chosen value would print 0 and announce itself on the first run instead of silently
invalidating the record. Measured, for contrast: the non-dyadic quotient 1.0L / 3.0L printed at 25
fractional digits gives a different value on the two x87 targets than on the two binary128 targets.

THE FORMAT THE ROUND TRIP RELIES ON IS ESTABLISHED, NOT ASSERTED BY WIDTH. sizeof(double) == 8
establishes that the type occupies eight bytes and nothing more; it establishes neither the radix nor
the significand width, so width alone could not carry this claim. The documented basis for the format
is each target's own ABI -- the System V i386 and x86-64 psABIs,
AAPCS64 and the RISC-V calling convention all specify IEEE binary64 for double -- and the property is
CHECKED at run time by the double_significand line, because a floating comparison is not an integer
constant expression and C11 6.6p6 keeps it out of a static assertion. The probe adds 1/2^52 and 1/2^53
to 1.0 through a volatile double staging object: the first increment must be resolved and the second,
which lies exactly half-way below it, must be absorbed by round-to-nearest-even, and together they pin
the significand at 53 radix-2 bits. The staging object is volatile and of type double for one reason
each and both are essential -- C11 6.3.1.8p2 removes extra range and precision on assignment, and the
volatile qualification makes the store and the reload actually happen -- without which the two x87
targets would evaluate the sums in wider registers and report a different answer from the other two,
a difference in the test rather than in any compiler.

EXCESS INTERMEDIATE PRECISION CANNOT MOVE ANY VALUE THIS PROGRAM PRINTS, AND THAT IS A CONSEQUENCE OF
THE EXACTNESS ABOVE RATHER THAN A SEPARATE HOPE. i686 is the one target measured with
__FLT_EVAL_METHOD__ == 2 -- x87 evaluates float and double expressions in the wider long double
format -- while x86_64, aarch64 and riscv64 all measure 0. C11 5.2.4.2.2p9 sanctions exactly that,
and 6.3.1.8p2 permits an implementation to carry extra range and precision through an intermediate
result. Neither can shift a printed digit here: every operand and every result of every operation is
exactly representable in binary64 and therefore in the 64-bit and 113-bit significands too, so no
operation has a rounding decision available to take, and evaluating an exact computation in a wider
format returns that same exact value. The one place a wider evaluation WOULD be observable is the
significand probe, whose entire question is about the double format, and that is exactly why its
staging object is a volatile double rather than an expression: the assignment discards the extra
precision 6.3.1.8p2 permits, and the volatile qualification makes the discarding actually happen.

NO NON-FINITE VALUE, NO NEGATIVE ZERO AND NO SUBNORMAL APPEARS, and identity properties of those
values are not what this program tests. The subnormal threshold in particular is where the two long
double formats part company: MEASURED, the least positive value is 3.645200e-4951 in x87 80-bit
extended against 6.475175e-4966 in IEEE binary128, so a program that went near it would be reading a
target-dependent boundary. Nothing here goes anywhere near either. Every magnitude computed or printed
sits far inside the finite range of all three representations and vastly above every subnormal
threshold: the largest is 1234567 and the smallest non-zero is one eighth, against a finite maximum
measured at 1.189731e+4932 on all four targets, so no operation approaches overflow, underflow or
gradual underflow anywhere. No division has a zero divisor. The conversions to an integer type
truncate 6.25, -6.25 and 12.5, so each integral part sits far inside its destination range on every
target, which is what keeps a floating-to-integer conversion defined under C11 6.3.1.4p1.

NO OBJECT REPRESENTATION IS INSPECTED, AND THE REASON IS NOT THAT INSPECTING ONE WOULD BE UNDEFINED.
Character-type access to an object representation is PERMITTED: C11 6.2.6.1p4 defines the object
representation as an array of unsigned char, and 6.5p7 lets an lvalue of character type alias any
object, so reading a long double byte by byte is well defined and this record does not claim
otherwise. What such a read would not be is a portable oracle. A 12-byte or 16-byte object of this
type carries padding beyond its significant bytes; those padding bytes take unspecified values that
nothing in this program sets or controls, C11 6.2.6.1p6 makes the value of the padding in a structure
unspecified for the same reason, and both the extent of the padding and the encoding of the
significant bytes were measured to differ across the four targets. A byte image would therefore
differ between two backends for reasons that say nothing about either compiler, which is exactly the
kind of comparison this suite must not make. So the program inspects none of it: no long double
object is examined byte by byte, copied into an object of another type, or reinterpreted through
overlapping storage or a pointer cast, and no assertion anywhere depends on a padding byte. No
`LDBL_` characteristic macro is named anywhere in the program either -- not the significand width,
the epsilon, the maximum or the exponent range -- and no header is included from which one could
come, so nothing target-dependent about the type's own description can reach a comparison.

EVERY CONVERSION IS EXPLICIT, EVERY LITERAL CARRIES THE TYPE IT MEANS, AND EVERY ARGUMENT MATCHES THE
CONVERSION SPECIFICATION THAT PRINTS IT. The three are one discipline, because a lapse in each is
punished differently -- by the gate, by the value, and by the standard. Every conversion is written as
an explicit cast: long double to double, to float, to int and to long long, and int, long long and
double to long double. Nothing narrows implicitly, and because this record deviates from no gate
member, -Wconversion and -Wsign-conversion are both ACTIVE, so an implicit narrowing would be rejected
before any comparison was reached rather than quietly compared. Every long double literal carries the
L suffix, with exactly two deliberate exceptions, each of which is the property its own line tests:
the unsuffixed 1.5, whose type is double because widening a double to a long double is what that pair
of lines measures, and the two dyadic quotients of the significand probe, which are double by
construction because the probe interrogates the double format. And every argument matches its
conversion specification exactly -- %.6Lf for a long double, %.6f for a double, %d for an int and
%lld for a long long, with the one float value converted to double by an explicit cast rather than
left to the default argument promotion -- because passing a long double where %f is written, or a
double where %Lf is written, is undefined behaviour under C11 7.21.6.1p9, and a printed digit produced
that way would say nothing about any compiler.

No signed integer overflow is possible. No shift appears. No object is read before it is written and no
object is modified twice between sequence points. Every volatile read is staged into a plain local in a
full expression of its own before the call that prints it -- the significand probe included -- so no
call receives a side-effecting argument and nothing depends on the unspecified order in which a call's
arguments are evaluated.

DETERMINISM. Every floating value printed is printed at a fixed precision with margin, and every
value in the program is a dyadic rational -- an integer, or an integer plus a multiple of one eighth,
the eighths being 3.125 and the accumulated 15.875 -- so it is exactly representable in binary32 and
binary64 alike and no rounding decision is ever taken. No non-finite value, no negative zero and no
subnormal appears anywhere, so no identity property of those values is relied on. No address, no
pointer value, no plain char value and no target-varying width is printed. Iteration order is fixed
by literal bounds. main returns 0, inside the 0-125 range the suite requires so that no exit status
is truncated.

HERMETICITY. Every input is a literal in this source. Nothing is opened, nothing is read from the
environment, the command line, the clock or the file system, and no storage is allocated.

GATE. The default warning gate applies unmodified and no `ub_audit_flags` key appears:
-Wall -Wextra -pedantic -Wconversion -Wsign-conversion -Wshadow -Werror. It is clean under that gate
in the single native compile-only invocation the suite makes of it, clean under the alternate
reference compiler this suite may select in its place, and clean under the one instrumented native
build of -fsanitize=undefined,address -fno-sanitize-recover=all. Both gates are
reference-compiler-only.

NO HEADER IS INCLUDED and printf is declared by hand, because bcc ships no standard I/O header.
END
impl_defined_notes <<END
ORACLE (b) IS DISABLED FOR THIS PROGRAM, AND THE MEASURED REASON IS THE POINT OF THE PROGRAM.

`long double` has no single representation across the four supported backends. MEASURED:
sizeof(long double) is 16 on x86_64, 12 on i686, 16 on aarch64 and 16 on riscv64; the two x86 targets
carry the x87 80-bit extended format inside that storage while the two others carry IEEE binary128.
Those are different formats, and the difference was measured rather than named: the significand is 64
bits on the two x86 targets against 113 on the other two, so the epsilon is 1.084202e-19 against
1.925930e-34 and the least positive value 3.645200e-4951 against 6.475175e-4966, which puts every
rounding boundary in the format at a different place. Note what did NOT differ, because a reason that
overstated itself would be no better than none: the normal exponent range and the finite maximum
measured identical on all four targets, at -16381 to 16384 and 1.189731e+4932, so it is the significand
and the rounding that follows from it -- not the reach of the exponent -- that the exclusion below
rests on. A value difference between two backends over this type is therefore an
IMPLEMENTATION-DEFINED difference, which is exactly the carve-out the requirements name -- a
cross-backend divergence not attributable to type widths, char signedness or the ABI is a defect, and
one that IS so attributable is not. Comparing the backends' long double values against one another
would report a permitted difference as a compiler defect, so cross-backend value equality is disabled
here and nowhere else in the corpus.

THE EXCLUSION IS AS NARROW AS THE MECHANISM ALLOWS, AND THE FEATURE IS NOT DROPPED. Constraint C3
forbids excluding a language feature because it is difficult, and nothing is excluded: the program is
written, compiled and executed on ALL FOUR targets at ALL THREE optimization levels, exactly like every
other program in the corpus. Oracle (a) remains enabled, so each backend's output is compared against
the SAME-TARGET reference compiler, which is the comparison that can actually judge it -- a reference
compiler for a given target uses that target's own long double format. Oracle (c) remains enabled, so
every cell is still asserted against the golden record, which catches drift and regression on every
target. What is given up is stated exactly, because understating it would be its own defect: disabling
oracle (b) suppresses ALL NINE of this program's cross-backend comparisons -- each of the three
non-baseline targets against the x86-64 baseline, at each of the three optimization levels. That is
one of the three oracles, for one of the 108 programs, and none of the other 107; it is given up
because those nine comparisons have no meaning over a type with three representations, rather than
because they are inconvenient.

THE GOLDEN RECORD HOLDS ON FOUR TARGETS ANYWAY, WHICH IS WHY ORACLE (c) IS NOT NARROWED TOO. Every
printed value is exactly representable in double and is printed at fixed precision with margin, so all
twelve cells were MEASURED to produce these bytes identically. The exclusion above is therefore about
what the comparison would MEAN, not about a difference this program actually exhibits: the same values
printed by two different formats agree here because they were chosen to, and a program that printed a
non-dyadic long double result would not agree. Recording the restriction on the meaning rather than on
today's measurement is what keeps it correct if a later maintainer adds a value.

WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT PRINTED. sizeof(long double) never appears as an absolute value -- it is 16,
12, 16 and 16, so printing it would break the shared record on i686 immediately -- and exactly one
target-invariant relation about the type's width is printed instead. No long double characteristic
appears either. Two of them measured target-dependent and could not be printed at all: the significand
digit count, 64 against 113, and the epsilon, 1.084202e-19 against 1.925930e-34. Two measured identical
on all four targets and are still not printed -- the finite maximum, 1.189731e+4932, and the normal
exponent range, -16381 to 16384 -- because printing a characteristic of a type whose format is
target-defined would couple this record to a value that agrees today for reasons no line of this
program controls, and the record would then break on the first target whose format differed rather than
on the first defect. For the same reason no float.h macro is referenced; that header is bundled, but
every long-double characteristic macro it defines describes a target-defined format, and exercising the
bundled set is the job of 12_preprocessor/003_bundled_header_inclusion.c.

THE EXCLUSION CARRIES THE MARKER XD-TYPE-LONGDOUBLE-001, AND THE TWO ARE ONE STATEMENT RATHER THAN
TWO. The oracle (b) toggle above says WHICH comparison is not made; the marker block below says on
whose authority, under which identifier, and over which cells -- and it is what puts this exclusion
into tests/conformance/EXPECTED_DIVERGENCES.md where the register's bidirectional audit can see it.
A reasoned narrowing with a marker is auditable in one place; the same narrowing justified by prose
alone is auditable nowhere, and it would still be reported XFAIL, so the verdict would claim the
authority of a documented exclusion that no identifier and no resolved basis stood behind. The record
format now refuses that state: a record that disables an oracle without a marker scoping it is
rejected at parse time.

THE MARKER'S CLASS IS `comparison_excluded`, AND THAT IS THE HALF OF THIS RECORD MOST RECENTLY
CORRECTED. It used to read `stdout_mismatch`, which is one of the six classes a real comparison can
produce, and writing it here asserted in the one field a report row quotes that two completed runs had
disagreed on bytes. Nothing ran: the comparison this marker scopes is the one the record above
switches off. Every automated check passed anyway, because a class is only ever matched against an
observation and this arm produces none -- so the false statement sat in a machine-checked field that
no machine could contradict. `comparison_excluded` is the class that says what is true, it is accepted
only on an arm the record disables, and an observational class is now refused on such an arm and this
one refused on an arm that is compared: the parser holds both directions, so the class and the oracle
toggle can no longer drift apart. Keeping the identifier, the scope and the resolved basis unchanged
is deliberate -- what needed fixing was the kind of thing the marker claimed, not its authority.

WHAT THE MARKER CLAIMS, PRECISELY. Not that the four backends disagree today -- they do not; all
twelve cells print these bytes, because every printed value was chosen to be exact in all three
representations. Nor does it claim that any comparison saw anything: its class names no observation.
What it claims is that a cross-backend VALUE comparison over this type could not be read as evidence
about bcc even if they did disagree, because the four backends do not share one encoding: two of them
hold x87 80-bit extended, in 16 bytes and in 12, and the other two hold IEEE binary128 in 16 -- three
storage-and-format pairings over two distinct formats, each of which rounds at a different place. That
is a statement about what the comparison would MEAN, which is why it stays correct if a later
maintainer adds a value that does diverge -- and it is why the marker can never reach XPASS: nothing
is compared on the arm it scopes, so no divergence can disappear from it.

RETIREMENT. Re-enabling oracle (b) here means deleting the marker block, the register entry and the
impl_defined_notes reason in one edit; narrowing any oracle without all three fails the run, which is
the format declining to let an exclusion become silent.

WIDTH NORMALIZATION ELSEWHERE. The only integer conversions printed are to int and to long long, both
of which have the same width on all four targets, so no target-varying integer width reaches the output
either.
END
expected_stdout    <<END
ld_add=3.750000 ld_sub=1.250000 ld_mul=3.125000 ld_div=1.250000
ld_neg=-2.500000 ld_chain=4.500000 ld_accum=15.875000
ld2d=3.125000 ld2f=2.500000 d2ld=1.500000 i2ld=42.000000
ld2i=6 ld2i_neg=-6 ld2ll=12 ll2ld=1234567.000000
ld_lt=1 ld_gt=1 ld_le_equal=1 ld_ge_equal=1
ld_lt_equal=0 ld_eq=1 ld_ne_equal=0 ld_ne_diff=1
rt_add=3.750000 rt_sub=1.250000 rt_mul=3.125000 rt_div=1.250000
rt_neg=-2.500000 rt_chain=4.500000 rt_accum=15.875000
rt_ld2d=3.125000 rt_ld2f=2.500000 rt_d2ld=1.500000 rt_i2ld=42.000000
rt_ld2i=6 rt_ld2i_neg=-6 rt_ld2ll=12 rt_ll2ld=1234567.000000
rt_lt=1 rt_gt=1 rt_le_equal=1 rt_ge_equal=1
rt_lt_equal=0 rt_eq=1 rt_ne_equal=0 rt_ne_diff=1
folded_matches_runtime=1 1 1 1 1 1 1
folded_matches_runtime_conv=1 1 1 1 1 1 1 1
folded_matches_runtime_cmp=1 1 1 1 1 1 1 1
exact_in_double=1 1 1 1 1 1 1
double_significand=1 1
ld_at_least_double=1
END
expected_divergence.id       = XD-TYPE-LONGDOUBLE-001
expected_divergence.class    = comparison_excluded
expected_divergence.scope    = oracle_b; all targets; all opt levels
expected_divergence.basis    = docs/technical-specifications.md, line 511, the type representation is specified with target-parametric sizes covering the floating types including long double
expected_divergence.documented <<END
Type representation with target-parametric sizes
END
expected_divergence.observed <<END
NO CROSS-BACKEND VALUE COMPARISON IS ATTEMPTED FOR THIS PROGRAM: the record above disables oracle (b),
and this marker's class is comparison_excluded precisely so that this field describes no comparison
result. What was observed is a measurement of the TYPE, made with the four pinned reference drivers:
sizeof(long double) is 16 on x86_64, 12 on i686, 16 on aarch64 and 16 on riscv64, with the two x86
targets carrying the x87 80-bit extended format inside that storage and the other two carrying IEEE
binary128 -- three storage-and-format pairings over two distinct formats, whose significands are 64
bits against 113 and which therefore round at different places. Measured beside it: the twelve cells
this program does run print byte-identical stdout, because every value printed was chosen to be exact
in all three representations, so the exclusion is about what a cross-backend value comparison over
this type would MEAN rather than about any difference this program exhibits. A value difference
between two backends over this type is an implementation-defined difference of the kind the brief's
carve-out names, not a defect in either.
END
